<instruction name="FYL2X">
 <copyright>Copyright (C) Intel Corporation. All Rights Reserved.</copyright>
 <shortdesc>Compute y * log2x</shortdesc>
 <encoding_table><table unique="1261193">
   <thead><tr>
     <th><para unique="1261195">Opcode</para>
      </th>
     <th><para unique="1261197">Instruction</para>
      </th>
     <th><para unique="1261199">64-Bit Mode</para>
      </th>
     <th><para unique="1261201">Compat/&#8232;Leg Mode</para>
      </th>
     <th><para unique="1261203">Description</para>
      </th>
     </tr>
    </thead><col width="82.65096pt"/>
   <col width="98.54495999999999pt"/>
   <col width="48.25224pt"/>
   <col width="59.649840000000005pt"/>
   <col width="223.38504pt"/>
   <tbody>
    <tr>
     <td><para unique="1261205">D9 F1</para>
      </td>
     <td><para unique="1261207">FYL2X</para>
      </td>
     <td><para unique="1261209">Valid</para>
      </td>
     <td><para unique="1261211">Valid</para>
      </td>
     <td><para unique="1261213">Replace ST(1) with (ST(1) * log<subscript>2</subscript>ST(0)) and pop the register stack.</para>
      </td>
     </tr>
    </tbody>
   </table>
  </encoding_table>
 <longdesc><para unique="1261216">Computes (ST(1) * log<subscript>2</subscript> (ST(0))), stores the result in register ST(1), and pops the FPU register stack. The source operand in ST(0) must be a non-zero positive number.</para>
  <para unique="1261217">The following table shows the results obtained when taking the log of various classes of numbers, assuming that neither overflow nor underflow occurs.</para>
  <table unique="1261218">
